.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "why", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 1. Why Factories Over Fixtures
  :markdown
    Rails fixtures are a global, shared dataset: every test sees the same
    `users.yml`, so the record one test needs is the record another test
    is counting, and a change to a fixture ripples through specs that never
    mention it. Factories flip that around. Each test **declares** the
    objects it needs, right where it needs them, and gets fresh ones nobody
    else can touch.

    The discipline that makes this work is the **minimal valid record**: a
    factory defines *only* what the model needs to be valid, and every
    other attribute is opt-in at the call site. When a spec reads
    `create(:user, :admin, name: "Ada")`, everything that matters to that
    spec is on that line — and nothing that doesn't matter is hiding in a
    file three directories away.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "install", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 2. Installation
  :markdown
    Add `factory_bot_rails` to the development and test group so the
    generators know to create factory files, then include the syntax
    methods in RSpec so you can write `create(:user)` instead of
    `FactoryBot.create(:user)`:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # Gemfile
      group :development, :test do
        gem "factory_bot_rails"
      end

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # spec/support/factory_bot.rb
      RSpec.configure do |config|
        config.include FactoryBot::Syntax::Methods
      end

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    Factories live in `spec/factories/`, one file per model, and
    `factory_bot_rails` loads them automatically. A first factory looks
    like this — note that every attribute is a block, so values are
    evaluated fresh for each record:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # spec/factories/users.rb
      FactoryBot.define do
        factory :user do
          name  { "Ada Lovelace" }
          email { generate(:email) }
        end
      end

= doc_note(modifier: :note, title: "Does the File Get Required?", css: "mt-6") do
  :markdown
    A generated `rails_helper.rb` has the `spec/support` glob commented out.
    Uncomment `Rails.root.glob("spec/support/**/*.rb").sort.each { |f| require f }`
    (or add the include directly to `rails_helper.rb`) or the syntax methods
    silently won't be there.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "strategies", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 3. Build Strategies
  :markdown
    Four verbs make objects from a factory, and choosing the cheapest one
    that satisfies the test is the single biggest lever on suite speed.
    They all accept the same traits and attribute overrides.

- # The strategy table: the verb, what it touches, and when to use it.
.mt-4.overflow-x-auto.rounded-box.border.border-base-300
  = daisy_table(css: "table-zebra") do |table|
    - table.with_head do |head|
      - head.with_column { "Verb" }
      - head.with_column { "What it touches" }
      - head.with_column { "Reach for it when" }
    - strategies.each do |verb, touches, use|
      - table.with_row do |row|
        - row.with_column do
          %code= verb
        - row.with_column do
          :markdown
            #{touches}
        - row.with_column do
          :markdown
            #{use}

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    Each verb has a `_list` form (`create_list(:user, 3)`) and a `_pair`
    form. Overrides are a trailing hash, and a block receives the object
    after it is built:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      user   = build(:user, name: "Grace")           # unsaved
      admins = create_list(:user, 2, :admin)          # saved, with a trait
      params = attributes_for(:user)                  # { name: "...", email: "..." }

      create(:user) do |user|
        user.posts.create!(attributes_for(:post))
      end

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "traits", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 4. Traits
  :markdown
    A trait is a named bundle of attributes that describes a **variation** of
    the record — `:admin`, `:expired`, `:with_avatar`. They replace the two
    ways factories go wrong: one enormous factory that sets everything, and
    a pile of boolean flags (`admin: true, active: true, verified: true`)
    that every spec has to get right in combination.

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # spec/factories/subscriptions.rb
      FactoryBot.define do
        factory :subscription do
          user
          plan       { "starter" }
          expires_at { 1.month.from_now }

          trait :expired do
            expires_at { 1.day.ago }
          end

          trait :enterprise do
            plan  { "enterprise" }
            seats { 50 }
          end
        end
      end

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      create(:subscription, :expired)
      create(:subscription, :enterprise, :expired, seats: 12)   # traits compose; overrides win

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    Traits are listed as symbols before the overrides hash, compose in any
    combination, and work with every build strategy. If you find yourself
    passing the same three traits together everywhere, that is a new trait.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "associations", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 5. Associations and Sequences
  :markdown
    Writing the association's name on its own line — the bare `user` in the
    subscription factory above — builds it from the `:user` factory using
    **the same strategy as the parent**: `build(:subscription)` builds an
    unsaved user, `create(:subscription)` creates one. That default (since
    factory_bot 5) is what keeps `build` from quietly hitting the database
    through its associations. Point at a different factory or trait with
    `association`:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      factory :subscription do
        association :user, factory: [:user, :admin]   # explicit factory + trait
      end

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    **Sequences** exist for uniqueness constraints. A factory that hardcodes
    `email { "ada@example.com" }` works exactly once per test; a sequence
    hands out a fresh value every time it is asked:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # spec/factories/sequences.rb
      FactoryBot.define do
        sequence(:email) { |n| "person\#{n}@example.com" }
      end

      # Or inline, scoped to one factory:
      factory :user do
        sequence(:username) { |n| "user_\#{n}" }
      end

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "transients", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 6. Transient Attributes and Callbacks
  :markdown
    A **transient** attribute is a factory input that is not a model
    attribute. Combined with `after(:build)` / `after(:create)` callbacks it
    lets a factory take *instructions* — "give this list three items" —
    without the spec having to build the items itself:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # spec/factories/lists.rb
      FactoryBot.define do
        factory :list do
          title { "Groceries" }

          transient do
            items_count { 0 }
          end

          after(:create) do |list, evaluator|
            create_list(:item, evaluator.items_count, list: list)
            list.reload
          end
        end
      end

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      create(:list)                  # no items
      create(:list, items_count: 3)  # three items, associated and reloaded

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    The callback receives the record and an **evaluator** holding every
    attribute including the transient ones. Transients are ignored by
    `attributes_for` (they aren't model attributes), and an `after(:create)`
    callback only fires for `create` — if the variation has to work with
    `build` and `build_stubbed` too, prefer an inline association list
    (`items { [association(:item)] }`), which every strategy honors.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "hygiene", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 7. Suite Speed and Hygiene
  :markdown
    Factories are the easiest way to make a suite slow, because the cost is
    invisible at the call site. Three habits keep it in check.

    **Persist only when persistence is the point.** A spec of a model
    method, a presenter, or a component rarely needs a database row. Reach
    for `build` or `build_stubbed` first and step up to `create` when the
    test actually queries. Across hundreds of examples this is the
    difference between seconds and minutes.

    **Watch for association explosions.** A `create(:comment)` that builds a
    post, which builds an author, which builds an organization, which
    builds a billing plan, quietly inserts half the schema per example.
    Keep required associations minimal in the factory, and lean on
    `build_stubbed` where the chain doesn't matter.

    **Lint the factories.** A factory that no longer produces a valid
    record fails every spec that uses it, usually with a message about the
    spec rather than the factory. `FactoryBot.lint` builds every factory
    (and, with `traits: true`, every trait) and reports the broken ones by
    name. Run it as a rake task in CI rather than `before(:suite)`, so
    single-spec runs stay fast:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # lib/tasks/factory_bot.rake
      namespace :factory_bot do
        desc "Verify that every factory and trait builds a valid record"
        task lint: :environment do
          if Rails.env.test?
            ActiveRecord::Base.transaction do
              FactoryBot.lint traits: true
              raise ActiveRecord::Rollback
            end
          else
            system("bundle exec rake factory_bot:lint RAILS_ENV=test") || abort
          end
        end
      end

.max-w-prose
  = daisy_code(css: "my-4", prefix: "$") do
    bundle exec rake factory_bot:lint

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "when", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 8. When a Factory Is Overkill
  :markdown
    Factories describe **persistable records with validity rules**. Not
    every object a test needs is one of those:

    - A **plain Ruby object** — a value object, a form object, a service's
      input — is clearer constructed directly: `Money.new(1200, "USD")`
      says more than `build(:money)` ever will.
    - A **params hash** for a request spec is often just a literal hash. Use
      `attributes_for` when the factory already knows the valid shape;
      write the hash when the spec is *about* the shape.
    - A **one-off record** with unusual attributes belongs in the spec as
      overrides, not as a new factory or trait nobody else will use.

    The question to ask is whether the next reader learns more from the
    factory name or from the literal. Factories earn their keep when the
    same valid record is needed in many places; the rest of the time, plain
    Ruby wins.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "success", class: "scroll-mt-24" } Success!
  :markdown
    Your test data is declared where it is used, minimal by default, and as
    fast as each spec allows. Before you move on:

